- Review history

Use a versioned and extensible schema.

Do not unnecessarily duplicate the existing anatomical knowledge records.

## 4.2 Scientific provenance

Introduce a proper source attribution and evidence system.

Distinguish clearly between:

A. Geometry provenance:
Where the actual 3D anatomical geometry originated.

B. Anatomical ontology provenance:
Where a structure identifier, name, hierarchy or classification originated.

C. Educational evidence provenance:
Which authoritative sources support the educational explanations and anatomical relationships.

D. External educational resources:
Where students can independently study the topic.

These are different forms of provenance and must not be conflated.

Each relationship should support an individual evidence record with fields such as:

- Source title
- Author or institution
- Publisher
- Edition
- Publication date
- Chapter or relevant page
- DOI when applicable
- Direct URL
- Source access date
- Supported anatomical claim
- Review status
- Reviewer, when genuinely known
- Review date, when genuinely performed

Create explicit content statuses, such as:

- Draft
- Needs evidence
- Under review
- Verified
- Needs revision
- Deprecated

Do not automatically classify every generated relationship as verified.

Do not use a generic source name to imply that every individual fact has been independently checked.

Never fabricate authors, publication dates, DOI identifiers, pages, reviewers or expert approval.

## 4.3 Scientific review pipeline

Build a reusable validation pipeline that checks:

- Invalid anatomical identifiers
- Missing source records
- Broken source associations
- Inconsistent relationship directions
- Orphaned references
- Invalid hierarchy links
- Missing geometry mappings
- Concept-only structures incorrectly represented as modeled
- Contradictions between knowledge records and learning records
- Duplicate or conflicting terminology
- Missing educational explanations
- Missing review metadata

Allow future content contributors or qualified medical reviewers to extend the content without modifying core application components.

Add machine-checkable quality rules, but never treat automated validation as a substitute for medical review.

